# M4 client-plane probe — raw evidence

> Command: `node deepblend/tools/ui-loop-probe.mjs --url "http://127.0.0.1:3099/?token=…"`
> Process: a second `dsh web` (port 3099) started from this checkout, so the
> running GUI on 3080 and this session were left untouched.
> Date: 2026-09-13

## 1. The measurement that decides the development loop

```
[PASS] the client bundle registered a sidebar entry in a real page — aria-label="Blender"
[PASS] the icon the plugin drew is in the DOM
[PASS] selecting the entry renders the main panel — panel text="Blender 5.2.1 LTS"
[PASS] the panel shows a fact fetched from the Host over HTTP, same origin — Blender 5.2.1 LTS
[PASS] editing one line reaches an OPEN page with no refresh and no restart — 610ms after the write
[PASS] the update happened in the SAME document — a swap, not a silent reload — window marker survived (set at 1789311690032)

verdict: A-never-happens: the client-plugin reload chain swaps the fiber with no refresh and no restart
```

A second run measured **713 ms**. The one line edited was
`const PROBE_LABEL = 'Blender'` → `'Blender edit-1'` in
`packages/deepblend/ui/lib/client.js`; the page was a real (headless) Chrome
holding a real session against the real Host.

Why this is the right question and not a curiosity: with a bundler, every later
UI iteration would have cost a build; with a restart, ~10 s each; this is ~0.6 s
against a page that is already open, so the M4 UI is written the same way the
rest of this repository is written — source is the artifact — and no build step
was introduced.

A third run — after the probe was hardened (see §5) — measured **723 ms**, and the same
six facts held:

```
[PASS] the client bundle registered a sidebar entry in a real page — aria-label="Blender"
[PASS] the icon the plugin drew is in the DOM
[PASS] selecting the entry renders the workbench panel
[PASS] the panel reads the Host over HTTP, same origin, on a declared route
[PASS] editing one line reaches an OPEN page with no refresh and no restart — 723ms after the write
[PASS] the update happened in the SAME document — a swap, not a silent reload — window marker survived
[PASS] the swap closes a panel that was selected (the shell drops an id that is briefly unregistered)

verdict: A-never-happens: the client-plugin reload chain swaps the fiber with no refresh and no restart
```

The seventh check is a consequence worth knowing before it is mistaken for a bug: after a
swap the workbench **panel** closes (the sidebar entry stays). The shell's layout
deselects a `main` key that is momentarily unregistered, so a one-line edit while the
panel is open sends you back to the conversation column. One click reopens it.

## 2. What makes that work (read from the deployment, not assumed)

* `dsh-client-hmr` (node half) is mounted unconditionally by the shipped
  `dsh-web-app` bundle. Every 500 ms it `stat`s each graph row's client bundle
  (`dsh-client-hmr/lib/index.js`, `pollIntervalMs` default 500) and on an
  mtime/size change calls `clientModules.rebuilt(id)`.
* `rebuilt()` re-reads the file, recomputes the content revision, recomposes the
  graph, and notifies `onRebuilt` listeners.
* The same row serves `GET /plugins/events` (SSE) and pushes a `rebuilt` frame;
  the browser half invalidates, prefetches, and swaps the fiber **without
  navigating**. That is what the surviving `window` marker above proves.

## 3. The two changes that are NOT free (measured, same session)

**3.1 Declaring `dsh.client` for the first time needs one restart.**
`dsh-client-modules` caches each loader row's package verdict — including the
negative "not a client package" verdict — per `(baseUrl, specifier)` until
restart. Measured against the process that was already running: after
`packages/deepblend/ui/package.json` gained `dsh.client` + `./client`:

```
Slots.listSubTree { root: "sidebar.panellist" }  →  occupants: []      (live page, port 3080)
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:3099/ | grep -o 'deepblend[^"]*'
  @deepblend/dsh-blender-ui
  @deepblend/dsh-blender-ui/client.js                                    (fresh process, port 3099)
```

**3.2 A Host-half edit needs a restart.** `packages/deepblend/ui/lib/index.js`
was edited to serve `card.title = 'Blender HOST-EDIT-PROBE'`, then:

```
served title now: Blender
```

The running process kept serving the old bytes. Node's ESM module cache is
process-level and not clearable, which is the same fact M0 §8.1 and
`milestone-status.md` §12.9 recorded — re-measured here because the whole M4
loop depends on which half you are editing.

## 4. What this means for M4

| Edited | To see it |
|---|---|
| `packages/deepblend/ui/lib/client.js` (the UI) | save — it is live in every open page in about half a second |
| `packages/deepblend/ui/package.json` (`dsh.client`, exports) | restart once |
| any Host-half module (`ui/lib/index.js`, `host/…`, `contracts/…`) | restart `dsh web` |

Consequence for verification, and the reason the M4 acceptance suite is shaped
the way it is: the client half can be verified by a browser that reloads nothing,
while the Host half has to be verified in a process that was started *after* the
change. Both are automated — see `deepblend/tests/e2e/ui.e2e.mjs`, which boots
its own `dsh web` for exactly this reason.

## 5. What the probe itself got wrong first (and why that matters)

The first hardened version of this tool reported `verdict: B: the running process must be
restarted` — three times in a row, against a deployment where the loop demonstrably worked.
The page said why, once the probe started listening to its console:

```
[page exception] ReferenceError: PANEL_LABEL is not defined
[page error]     locale subscriber crashed: ReferenceError: PANEL_LABEL is not defined
[page error]     Error: failed to apply loader entry … (@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-ui-sidebar):
                 PANEL_LABEL is not defined
```

The cause was the probe's own write, not the deployment: it saved the file with
`writeFileSync`, which truncates first. `dsh-client-hmr` polls that exact file every 500 ms,
so every so often it read the file **mid-save** and rebuilt the bundle from a short read —
and the page then swapped to a bundle whose entry did not apply.

Two things follow, and both are recorded rather than smoothed over:

1. **A save must be atomic.** The probe now writes a temporary file and renames it, which is
   what editors do and what the dev chain needs. With that change the measurement is stable
   (three consecutive runs, 610 / 713 / 723 ms). A truncating save is a real hazard for the
   loop, and its symptom is not "a stale label" — it is the panel and the sidebar entry
   disappearing together.
2. **A probe that reports its own bug as a product verdict is worse than no probe.** The
   first failure was reported as "the running process must be restarted", which is exactly
   the kind of confident wrong answer this repository keeps refusing. The tool now listens to
   the page's console and says when the page lost the plugin to a failed swap, separately
   from "HMR did not happen".
